# layout.zd — the two components `blog.zd` composes its pages out of.
#
# A module is a unit of naming rather than of deployment (§14D.2), so a
# component may live in one file and the view that uses it in another.
# Nothing here has a placement: a component is colorless and runs wherever
# its inputs are.

# The page frame. `children` is the slot the caller's body lands in, which
# is what makes `PageShell` a shell rather than a fixed page.
#
# The measure is set here and nowhere else. `maxWidth is 720` is the
# roughly 70-character line long-form text is readable at, and
# `lineHeight is 1.6` is the leading that goes with it; both are on the
# frame rather than on each post, because a measure that every card had to
# repeat is a measure that one card will eventually get wrong. The line
# height is unitless deliberately: a multiple is inherited as a multiple,
# so a heading inside the frame gets leading proportional to its own size
# rather than to the body's.
component PageShell with title, children
    Column maxWidth is 720, lineHeight is 1.6
        Heading title
        children

# One post. The heading and the slug are the card's own; the rendered body
# is passed in as `children`, because a card that knew how to render a
# post would be a second place that has to agree with the build about what
# a post is.
#
# That the body arrives as `children` is also what makes it renderable.
# `Prose` takes no element children — interleaving parsed nodes with
# templated ones would make sibling offsets depend on a file's parse — so
# the document has to be a *sibling* of the title and the slug rather than
# a wrapper around them. This `Column` is what makes them siblings, and it
# is why the caller writes `Prose post.body` under the card rather than
# the card writing `Prose` around whatever it was given.
component PostCard with slug, title, children
    Column
        Heading title
        Text slug
        children
